<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN"
        "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd">

<html>
  <head>
    <title>Machiavelli - Order (and re-order) cards til you have no more</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Documentation of the Machiavelli cardgame">
    <meta name="keywords" content="Documentation, Docu, Machiavelli, game, card, cardgame">

    <meta name="DC.Creator" content="Markus Schwab">
    <meta name="DC.Date" content="2003-11-30">
    <meta name="DC.Rights" content="Copyright (C) 2003 - 2006, distributed under the GNU Free Documentation License">
  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">Machiavelli</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>I learned Machiavelli from a swiss bloke, who itself learned it from
      italiens (which in turn might have learned it from someone else). And
      actually I'm not even sure, if the name is right ...</p>

    <p>Anyway, it is a shedding-type card game played by 4 sets of
      52-card decks.</p>

    <h2>Dealing</h2>
    <p>Every player starts with 7 cards in his hand. The remaining
      ones are put into the deck.</p>

    <p>A randomly selected player starts the game; the others start
      the next ones in turn.</p>

    <h2>Playing</h2>
    <p>In the beginning of the turn the player takes a card from the
      deck (this happens automatically).

    <p>Then he can put down "matching" cards &mdash; either making a
      new pile on the table or adding cards to existing piles &mdash;
      and that way get rid of its cards. "Matching" means that either
      the numbers must be equal (independend of the colour, but each
      colour only once) or the colour and the numbers are in a
      sequence (Ace (as one), two, three, ..., ten, jack, queen, king,
      ace).</p>

    <p>For that he can also use cards from existing piles, re-sort
      them however he likes, build new piles (even with cards from
      existing piles) - in a nutshell doing as he likes, as long as at
      the end of its turn all piles have at least 3 cards and follow
      the rules above.</p>

    <p>The cards are played and/or sorted with drag and drop<a
      href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>.  
    <p>If the player can't or won't continue, follows the next player
      still having cards (counter-clockwise). This end of the turn is
      indicated by clicking in the (accordingly named) button or in
      the pile of the reserve.</p>

    <p>The last player still having cards loses.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The deck: A heap of cards (with face down), from where you
        take up new cards (if you put the mouse over it you are
        informed about the remaining cards).</li>

      <li>The cards of the player. Presorted by colour, but
        re-sortable with drag and drop<a href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> or
	the menu.</li>

      <li>The piles on the table. Cards can be added to that piles
        with drag und drop<a href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> (if
        it is the turn of the player). Note that both cards from the hand of
        the player as cards already on the table can be moved.</li>

      <li>In the last row are the cards of the other players.</li>
     </ul>

    <hr size=1 noshade>
    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note1"></a>1)
      Drag and drop means clicking with the a mouse button on the card,
      holding the button and then dragging the card to its destination.
      The end of this activity is signaled by releasing the button.</p>

